#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "9ec1a675-6563-5374-85fd-871da49caf30" :question/math-academy-id "e-3727" :question/problem "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x = -\\sqrt 2$ and one local maximum at $x = \\sqrt 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/id #uuid "3f70f692-38d3-5b7e-9bbb-9fc089679fa0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3729" :question/problem "Find the stationary points of the function $f(x) =x^3$ and classify each point." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t=3x^2,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2 = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x = 0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =3(-1)^{2}=3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{2}=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, 0\\right)$ | $0$ | $\\left(0,\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |  | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nSo, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "a82def66-255f-5658-9109-ab7966855c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3730" :question/problem "Find the relative maximum and minimum points of the function $f(x) = (2x+3)e^x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(2x+3){e}^{x}+2{e}^{x}=(2x+5){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+5){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -5,0$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =(2(-5)+5){e}^{-5}=-\\frac{5}{{e}^{5}}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =(2(0)+5){e}^{0}=5>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty, -\\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ | $-\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $\\left( -\\dfrac{5}{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\nAt $x=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive.\n\nSo, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x = -\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f772e051-657c-598b-953d-aefbfeca8e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966} {:answer/id #uuid "95862677-e4f3-545a-8efd-e2a2122f2379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292967} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c5fc8d-7493-57d2-b12a-fd3be232c6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292968} {:answer/id #uuid "631dcd5d-8feb-5ea0-879b-8b738b9f80d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292969} {:answer/id #uuid "49731ccb-509a-5c99-8306-e8a84b760082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "631dcd5d-8feb-5ea0-879b-8b738b9f80d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292969} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b571ce7-3c29-5354-85c0-f9325e4fd427" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b49a83d0-be9c-5ea2-af93-457dbf50bb25" :question/math-academy-id "q-113750" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=12x+9{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12+18x-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6(2{x}^{2}-3x-2) \\\\  & =-6(x-2)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-6(x-2)(2x+1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},2.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-6(-1-2)(2(-1)+1)=-18<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0-2)(2(0)+1)=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6(3-2)(2(3)+1)=-42<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2})$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6412de-3305-5998-962d-82a19fb91481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292973} {:answer/id #uuid "c5de79bd-d4ad-5727-83f3-fb1c45b3528b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292974} {:answer/id #uuid "d34bc2f3-d2f5-5136-900d-dee8622b2d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292975} {:answer/id #uuid "3755b882-caad-5740-a693-c2910f9cd73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292976} {:answer/id #uuid "8be996ef-fde7-5ee9-b061-835bac1ce055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3755b882-caad-5740-a693-c2910f9cd73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292976} :answer-field/id #uuid "1149029e-4122-51cd-a2aa-0980a00ac29d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f661c07-7884-5002-810c-d6680fa001b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb20ecb-3cf6-58b5-bd9d-6b965a3762f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104257} {:answer/id #uuid "c4deb98e-cd6b-547a-acfc-ec16190f06c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104258} {:answer/id #uuid "d48460d1-1d9f-59d8-ad06-074f62eacc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104259} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0807b-7929-566e-938d-f243ec8608a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} :answer-field/id #uuid "840c6519-ee5d-5b70-b43a-17d5816bc817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273e570a-a467-5f2a-8bbc-0b66a3856ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49022" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b5ed1c8-16bc-5417-a259-3f27f7688a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293024} {:answer/id #uuid "036384c0-3ff8-5c09-9941-904335813c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} {:answer/id #uuid "0efdd25c-a9e2-55ab-a249-a953db365c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293026} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4fbafb-8a83-515e-98f4-a4ab95fcc039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293027} {:answer/id #uuid "33f4cd0b-b327-59f9-bfa1-c42341ea4659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "036384c0-3ff8-5c09-9941-904335813c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec30606-b998-52be-b98d-f8b320073278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a38627ef-e4b2-52dc-9751-9805f2cf3368" :question/math-academy-id "q-49035" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-5,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f8eee3b-af6e-5563-8933-55f19dc50656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293031} {:answer/id #uuid "7222a074-187f-59bd-a74d-5cfb174f1fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293032} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5c16c2-895e-521a-919a-e1e87c48f797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293033} {:answer/id #uuid "e5515bc2-998f-5f72-bc4c-2343a4dfee35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293034} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce8789c-445e-5549-93a6-70a74a3ce981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5515bc2-998f-5f72-bc4c-2343a4dfee35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293034} :answer-field/id #uuid "88615d9c-3523-584a-99fc-2013f6fabcd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cfa42c7e-dc08-577a-b09e-d8864c39ea66" :question/math-academy-id "q-79001" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=2+2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3}=2{x}^{2}(3-2x).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}(3-2x)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)^{2}(3-2(-1))=10>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{2}(3-2(1))=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)^{2}(3-2(2))=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
